{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:ac23157d989ba16beb6a5fef83e9e594352969e80e7b1ffeac4bad63046b9615","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.11,"B":0.5,"C":0.24,"D":0.15}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"I apply the lexical-priority lock strictly, using MY OWN CQ3 distribution and a 2032-36 OECD-38 GDP-weighted core inflation distribution derived from MY OWN CQ2.\n\nGROWTH MARGINAL, read off my CQ3 (P10 0.6 / P50 1.6 / P90 2.7, implied sigma ~0.82): P(g >= 2.6%) = 0.11; P(g < 2.0%) = Phi(0.49) = 0.69; P(g < 1.7%) = Phi(0.12) = 0.55.\n\nINFLATION MARGINAL FOR THE 2032-36 SUB-WINDOW. This must be derived from my CQ2 conjunct A (0.55 for >=2.50% on a 2027-36 average), and the two Wave-1 seats disagree on the profile's SHAPE, which is the one place I have to arbitrate rather than update. Fermi argues a RISING profile (care Baumol, non-tradable labour scarcity, and the interest claim as the old-age ratio goes 31% -> 40% all strengthen monotonically into the 2030s while Chinese goods deflation and AI services deflation are front-loaded), putting 2032-36 at ~2.55%. Scout argues the OPPOSITE (2027-29 is near-locked hot at ~2.9% by the observed OECD core of 3.6% and energy above 10%, so the later sub-window is COOLER than the ten-year mean). Both are right about their own term and they cancel to first order. I adopt central 2.45% with sigma 0.50, the sigma widened from CQ2's because the AI-Q4 capex-bust branch (66% locked) can produce a genuinely low-inflation digestion period in exactly these years. P(core >= 2.7%) = 0.31; P(core < 2.0%) = 0.18.\n\nSTEP 1 — A requires g >= 2.6%. A = 11. CONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED: my CQ3 P50 is 1.6% and my P90 is 2.7%, so A is a right-tail event and 11 is precisely P(g >= 2.6) implied by my own stated quantiles. The lock's worked example (a P50 of 2.1% forbidding most mass on A) applies to me a fortiori at 1.6%. I did NOT apply A's qualitative clauses (debt stabilizing via growth, broad wage gains), because rule 1 is a pure growth test; had I applied them — and my CQ1 of 9 says broad wage gains are unlikely — A would be lower, so 11 is an upper bound.\n\nSTEP 2 — C requires core >= 2.7% AND g < 2.0%. Unconditional product 0.69 x 0.31 = 0.214. I raise the conditional from 0.31 to 0.35 because in MY effort the binding constraints are supply walls, which make low growth and high core inflation the SAME event rather than opposing ones: energy inflation \"stabilised above 10%\", the US electrician deficit ~107,000 peaking 2027 against BLS shortfalls of ~81,000/yr THROUGH 2034, care vacancies clearing by rationing with Baumol cost disease against a strained public payer. 0.69 x 0.35 = 0.241 -> C = 24. Offsetting that lift, and why I do not go to Fermi's 28: the capex-bust branch produces low growth with LOW inflation, which populates D instead, so the two low-growth mechanisms partly cancel.\n\nSTEP 3 — D requires g < 1.7% AND core < 2.0%, disjoint from C by construction. Unconditional product 0.55 x 0.18 = 0.099. I raise the conditional from 0.18 to 0.28: that cell is over-populated by the AI-Q4 impairment branch (66% locked) landing as deflationary digestion, by BIS AER 2026 Box C's labour-income demand deficit, and by imported Chinese goods deflation (my own CQ2 conjunct B at 0.67). 0.55 x 0.28 = 0.154 -> D = 15. D also requires the bust's deflationary digestion to still be suppressing BOTH growth and inflation in 2032-36 rather than in 2028-31, which is roughly a one-in-four conditional and is what keeps D from running to 22.\n\nSTEP 4 — B is the residual: 100 - 11 - 24 - 15 = 50. SUM = 100 exactly.\n\nCONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ2, EXPLICITLY: my CQ2 = 32 is a JOINT (OECD hot AND China cold) and so must sit below my OECD-only conjunct-A marginal of 0.55; it does. That 0.55 for >=2.50% over 2027-36 maps, under the cancelling-profile argument above, to a 2032-36 central of 2.45%, which places 31% above C's higher 2.7% trigger — necessarily below 0.55 because the threshold is higher, which is the arithmetic link. Multiplied by P(g < 2.0) = 0.69 and lifted for supply-wall co-occurrence, that yields C = 24. C is therefore DERIVED from my CQ2 and my CQ3, not asserted. Sensitivity: had I used Fermi's rising-profile central of 2.55% with sigma 0.50, P(core >= 2.7) would be 0.36 and C would be 25; had I used Scout's cooler read with P(>=2.7) = 0.28, C would be 22. The answer is insensitive to that arbitration within +/-2 points, which is why I did not spend more credence on resolving it.\n\nTHE TAXONOMY FINDING, which the lock forces into the open and which I report as Fermi does: my modal cell is B at 50, but it is reached as a RESIDUAL at a growth rate of ~1.6%/yr — BELOW B's own stated 1.7-2.5% band — routed there because inflation sits between 2.0% and 2.7% and so triggers neither C nor D. Substantively that is still the bifurcated barbell (physical/care scarcity and hyperscaler rents coexisting with white-collar compression and steep real term premia), but half my mass sits in a cell whose LABEL implies about 1pp more growth than I forecast. Any aggregation that reads B as \"growth 1.7-2.5%\" will misread this payload.\n\nDIRECTION: relative to the Historian's prior (15/54/15/16), my updating MODERATED A down -4, AMPLIFIED C up +9, held D at -1, and moved B -4. The C move is the one that matters and it comes from the supply-wall conditioning, not from a higher inflation level — my inflation central of 2.45% is below Fermi's 2.55%.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.870Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD or national administrative earnings data show cumulative real median hourly wage growth in ISIC F + Q exceeding ISIC J + K + M by >= 4.0 percentage points over 2025-2030 — i.e. on pace for the 10.0pp CQ1 threshold — with the gap visible in a national statistical office series (not a job board) and NOT attributable to F+Q composition shifting toward physicians and skilled trades.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ1 conjunct B at 0.105 is wrong and the care shortfall is clearing by PRICE rather than by rationing. CQ1 moves from 9 to 22-26. Hypothesis plank (a) moves from 5 to ~18. CQ6-C moves up ~6 points (the fiscal transfer is being made, so the Baumol inflation channel is live) with CQ6-B down by the same. This is the most direct falsifier of my whole effort, because the 0.60 likelihood ratio I applied to the NSI vacancy-easing signal is the single largest discount in my CQ1 arithmetic.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's annualized real GDP growth in constant 2025 USD PPP averages >= 4.5%/yr over 2028-2031 on IMF WEO or World Bank data, with no reclassification of the PPP benchmark explaining it.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The locked -2.7pp/yr aging drag is not binding as I priced it, and the weight-times-drag product (-1.03pp of coupled growth) that carries my whole CQ3 update collapses. CQ3 P50 moves up +0.5pp to ~2.1%, P90 to ~3.1%. CQ6-A moves from 11 to ~22 and CQ6-D from 15 to ~9. Hypothesis plank (d) moves from 13 to ~25. Note this would also partially vindicate the simulation's China leg of 3.81%, which I discounted to LR 1.0 — so it falsifies my handling of the model evidence, not just my China number.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat / OECD AI-use surveys show the unweighted share of OECD-38 firms with >= 10 employees using AI directly in producing goods or services passing 35% — i.e. clearly on track for the AI-Q2 50% threshold — while PPP-weighted OECD TFP growth over the same period remains below +0.30pp/yr above the 2015-2022 baseline.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This CONFIRMS my scissors (adoption up, measured TFP flat) rather than falsifying it, so the falsifying branch is the mirror: if adoption is still BELOW 20% by end-2030, then labour scarcity is NOT the adoption driver I claim and my upward revision of AI-Q2 to ~30 is wrong, which removes the demand-pull leg of my coupling finding and pushes AI-Q1 down further on diffusion grounds instead of composition grounds. Either reading moves CQ6-B's composition, not its size. If instead BOTH adoption passes 35% AND TFP clears +0.50pp/yr, the wedge itself is false: CQ3 P50 up +0.4pp, CQ6-A to ~20, and my CQ6-B residual falls to ~42.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The share of newly licensed US RNs aged 40 and over rises above 15% (from the historical ~6-8%) in NCSBN or state board of nursing licensure data, OR total annual new RN + LPN licensures rise more than 25% above their 2025 level, in either case without a reduction in clinical placement requirements.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The stock-flow mismatch — the single mechanism behind my hypothesis plank (a) at 5 and a large part of CQ1's conjunct B — is clearing. The ~7% coverage arithmetic (0.12M/yr convertible against a 1.66M/yr need) would be wrong by a factor of two or more. Plank (a) moves from 5 to ~22; CQ1 to ~16; CQ6-C up ~4 as the care supply wall softens. This is a licensing-queue falsifier specifically, and it is the cleanest one, because licensure counts are published, dated and administrative — unlike the MOVE series I had to refuse.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"In the next MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >= 15%, G7 GDP-weighted 10-year nominal sovereign yields RISE by >= 25bp during the drawdown window (rather than falling >= 50bp), reported on benchmark closes.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My 0.36 conditional for P(bonds fail to hedge | drawdown) — shaded DOWN from the historical 3-of-8 = 0.38 on the argument that the modal drawdown here is a disinflationary AI capex bust — is wrong, and the regime has already switched. CQ5 moves from 18 to ~28 (conjunct B from 0.38 to ~0.55, carrying conjunct A up through the +0.45 correlation). CQ4's P90 moves from 3.3 to ~3.6 and the P50 up ~15bp, since the same regime is the eroding-convenience-yield/term-premium regime. CQ6-C up ~3 points. Conversely, a >= 25% drawdown in which G7 nominals fall >= 100bp would confirm my shade and take CQ5 to ~13.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 bayes seat, effort 'labor'."}}